# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# .gitattributes - one line-ending policy, so a checkout looks the same
# everywhere it is opened.
#
# WHY THIS FILE IS HERE
# ---------------------
# Until it existed, line endings depended on whoever last touched the file:
# core.autocrlf defaults to true on Windows and to unset on Linux, so the
# bytes git stored were correct by luck rather than by rule.  Two things
# depend on those bytes.
#
#   1. Streamlit Community Cloud clones on Linux.  It serves whatever is
#      in the object database, so a discrepancy would show up as the
#      deployed app differing from the machine that produced it.
#   2. test_data.csv and train_data.csv are the held-out split.  They are
#      required to be byte-identical in content, and that claim is only
#      checkable if the policy for writing them is written down.
#
# WHAT EACH RULE DOES
# -------------------
# `* text=auto` lets git detect text and store it as LF in the object
# database, while still converting on checkout for ordinary source.  Files
# git detects as binary (anything with NUL bytes in the first 8 KB) are
# left completely alone, so this rule cannot corrupt an artifact.
#
# The CSV rule is stricter on purpose: stored LF AND checked out LF, on
# every platform.  A data file's bytes should not depend on the OS of the
# person who happened to clone it.
#
# The binary rules are belt-and-braces.  They are redundant with text=auto
# today; they exist so that a future rule cannot quietly start rewriting a
# pickled model or a figure.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

* text=auto

# Data files: LF in the repository and LF on disk, everywhere.
*.csv text eol=lf

# Never normalize these, under any circumstances.
*.joblib binary
*.png binary
*.zip binary
